    <title>Estádio do Maracanã</title>
    <id>292965</id>
    <revision>
      <id>38242454</id>
      <timestamp>2006-02-05T02:10:59Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <username>Lesfer</username>
        <id>292021</id>
      </contributor>
      <minor />
      <text xml:space="preserve">: ''This page discusses the [[football (soccer)]] stadium, for other meanings, see ''[[Maracanã]].''

{{Football stadium |
  arenaname  = Maracanã |
  image      = [[Image:Maracana Stadium.jpg|250px]] |
  fullname   = Estádio Jornalista Mário Filho |
  nickname   = Maraca |
  built      = [[1950]] |
  opened     = [[16 June]] [[1950]] |
  capacity   = 103,022&lt;br/&gt;(77,720 seated) |
  homeof     = [[Botafogo de Futebol e Regatas|Botafogo FR]]&lt;br&gt;[[Clube de Regatas do Flamengo|CR Flamengo]]&lt;br /&gt;[[Fluminense Football Club|Fluminense FC]] |
  pitchsize  = 110 x 75 m |
}}

The '''Estádio do Maracanã''', also known as '''Maracanã''' stadium (official name: '''Estádio Jornalista Mário Filho''', Maracanã being its [[Maracanã (neighborhood)|neighborhood's name]]) in [[Rio de Janeiro]] ([[Brazil]]) is one of the [[List of football stadiums by capacity|biggest]] [[football (soccer)|football]] [[stadium]]s in the world, and it is home for three of the four biggest football clubs in Rio: [[Clube de Regatas do Flamengo|Flamengo]], [[Botafogo de Futebol e Regatas|Botafogo]] and [[Fluminense Football Club|Fluminense]].

The stadium, built for the {{Wc|1950}}, originally had a capacity of 200,000. However, its capacity was greatly reduced when it was converted to an all-seater in the [[1990s]]; it can currently hold around 77,720 seated and 103,022 with standing room. It is now undergoing renovations that will increase its all-seated capacity to around 100,000.

The 1950 final between Brazil and [[Uruguay national football team|Uruguay]] was hosted at the Maracanã stadium, and Brazil only needed a draw to win, but lost the match 2-1 after being 1-0 up; this match has since been known in [[South America]] as &quot;[[Maracanazo]]&quot;.

On [[January 22]], [[2006]], after nine months closed for reformation, the stadium was reopened. In the inaugural match, Botafogo beat [[Club de Regatas Vasco da Gama|Vasco da Gama]] 5-3. 

The [[Campeonato Carioca]] finals usually are played at Maracanã stadium. Also, the stadium hosted the [[FIFA Club World Championship 2000|2000 FIFA Club World Championship]] final between Vasco da Gama and [[Sport Club Corinthians Paulista|Corinthians]].

It is particularly famous for holding the fabled [[Fla-Flu]], the classic showdown between Flamengo and Fluminense, widely regarded as one of the most spectacular sporting events in the world.

Former Flamengo midfielder [[Zico]] is the stadium top scorer: 333 goals in 435 matches.

==Other stadium data==
*'''First game:''' [[Rio de Janeiro]] All-Stars 1x3 [[São Paulo]] All-Stars ([[16 June]], [[1950]])
*'''First goal scored by:''' [[Didi (football player)|Didi]] (Rio de Janeiro All-Stars)

==Trivia==
*The name ''Maracanã'' refers to a Brazilian kind of [[parrot]] which is found in the North of [[Brazil]].  
*The stadium was officialy named ''Jornalista Mário Filho'' because of a [[journalist]] who supported the stadium's construction, called Mário Filho. He was the brother of another famous Brazilian journalist and [[writer]], [[Nélson Rodrigues]].
*Maracanã is the stadium in which [[Pelé]] scored his 1,000th goal, in the match [[Club de Regatas Vasco da Gama|Vasco da Gama]] 1-2 [[Santos Futebol Clube|Santos]]. It was also the stadium where he made his debut with the [[Brazil national football team|Brazilian National Team]], against [[Argentina national football team|Argentina]], in [[1957]], and where he played his last game with the National Team, against [[Yugoslavia national football team|Yugoslavia]], in [[1971]]. Until nowadays, Pelé is the Brazilian National Team player who scored more goals playing at Marcanã: 30 goals in 22 games.    

==External links==
*{{pt icon}} [http://www.suderj.rj.gov.br/maracana/main.asp Official site of Maracanã]

