  <page>
    <title>Robert Spencer, 2nd Earl of Sunderland</title>
    <id>292967</id>
    <revision>
      <id>35322983</id>
      <timestamp>2006-01-15T22:40:09Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <username>Kusma</username>
        <id>145855</id>
      </contributor>
      <minor />
      <comment>disambiguation link repair ([[Wikipedia:Disambiguation pages with links|You can help!]]) -- [[United Provinces]]</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">'''Robert Spencer, 2nd Earl of Sunderland''' ([[1640]] &amp;ndash; [[September 28]], [[1702]]) was an [[England|English]] statesman and nobleman.

Born in [[Paris]], Spencer inherited his father's peerage dignities at the age of three, becoming '''Baron Spencer of Wormleighton''' and '''Earl of Sunderland'''. He joined the [[British Army]], reaching the rank of [[captain]] in the [[Prince Rupert Regiment of Horse]]. He married Anne Digby (d. [[1715]]), daughter of the [[George Digby, 2nd Earl of Bristol|Lord Bristol]] on [[10 June]] [[1665]], then proceeded to serve successively as [[ambassador]] to [[Madrid]] ([[1671]]&amp;ndash;[[1672]]), [[Paris]] (1672&amp;ndash;[[1673]]), and the [[Dutch Republic|United Provinces]] (1673). He served as a [[Gentleman of the Bedchamber]] from 1673 to [[1679]], then was invested a [[Privy Council of the United Kingdom|Privy Council]]lor and appointed [[Secretary of State for the Northern Department]] in 1679; at the same time, he served as Ambassador Extraordinary to Paris.

Lord Sunderland also served as [[Lord Lieutenant of Staffordshire]] during the minority of [[Charles Talbot, 1st Duke of Shrewsbury|Lord Shrewsbury]] until [[1681]]. That year, he was dismissed by [[Charles II of England|Charles II]], due to his opposition of the [[James II of England|Duke of York's]] succession, but presently regained the king's confidence (through his mistress, the [[Louise de Kérouaille, Duchess of Portsmouth|Duchess of Portsmouth]]). Intermittently, between [[1682]] and [[1688]], he served as [[Secretary of State for the Southern Department]], [[Lord Lieutenant]] of [[Warwickshire]], and [[Lord President of the Council]]; in [[1687]], he signed the King's grant of religious freedom for the Brenttown (Brenton) tract in Old Prince William County, Virginia to encourage settlement of French Protestants. The same year he openly embraced the [[Roman Catholicism|Roman Catholic]] faith to please the king. That year, he was also made a [[Order of the Garter|Knight of the Garter]]. However, it was clear that he was growing uncomfortable under the recently enthroned Duke of York (James II), and was summarily dismissed in October [[1688]], with the remark, &quot;You have your pardon; much good doe it you. I hope you will be more faithful to your next master than you have been to me.&quot;

Sunderland fled to [[Utrecht (city)|Utrecht]], the [[Netherlands]], and wrote to [[John Churchill, 1st Duke of Marlborough|Sir John Churchill]], a prominent English statesman, asking him to &quot;make things easy for a man in my condition.&quot; At first, King [[William III of England|William III]] excepted Lord Sunderland from the [[1690]] [[act of indemnity]], but by [[1691]], he was allowed to return to the country. He began sitting in the [[House of Lords]], and soon enough, King William paid a visit to his home in [[Althorp]], [[Northamptonshire]] to discuss public affairs. Lord Sunderland advised him to select all of his ministers from one political system, and eventually effected a reconciliation between King William and his sister-in-law, later Queen [[Anne of Great Britain|Anne]]. He was an influential advisor, causing William to accept only [[Whig]]s in his government.

Sunderland became [[Lord Chamberlain|Lord Chamberlain of the Household]] in April [[1697]], and was a [[Lord Justice]] for a short period, but &quot;the general suspicion with which he was regarded terrified him&quot;, and he eventually retired from public life in [[December]] of that year. Sunderland died in [[1702]] at Althorp, where he led a secluded life, and his only surviving son was [[Charles Spencer, 3rd Earl of Sunderland]].

{{start box}}
{{succession box | title=[[Secretary of State for the Northern Department]] | before=[[Joseph Williamson|Sir Joseph Williamson]] | after=[[Leoline Jenkins|Sir Leoline Jenkins]] | years=1679&amp;ndash;1680}}
{{succession box | title=[[Secretary of State for the Southern Department]] | before=[[Henry Coventry]] | after=[[Leoline Jenkins|Sir Leoline Jenkins]] | years=1680&amp;ndash;1681}}
{{succession box | title=[[Secretary of State for the Southern Department]] | before=[[Leoline Jenkins|Sir Leoline Jenkins]] | after=[[Charles Middleton, 2nd Earl of Middleton|The Earl of Middleton]] | years=1684&amp;ndash;1688}}
{{succession box | title=[[Lord President of the Council]] | before=[[George Savile, 1st Marquess of Halifax|The Marquess of Halifax]] | after=[[Thomas Osborne, 1st Duke of Leeds|The Marquess of Carmarthen]] | years=1685&amp;ndash;1688}}
{{succession box | title=[[Lord Chamberlain]] | before=[[Charles Sackville, 6th Earl of Dorset|The Earl of Dorset]] | after=[[Charles Talbot, 1st Duke of Shrewsbury|The Duke of Shrewsbury]] | years=1695&amp;ndash;1699}}
{{end box}}
{{start box}}
{{succession box | title=[[Earl of Sunderland]] | before=[[Henry Spencer, 1st Earl of Sunderland|Henry Spencer]] | after=[[Charles Spencer, 3rd Earl of Sunderland|Charles Spencer]] | years= }}
{{end box}}
==External links==
