  <page>
    <title>Kaitoke</title>
    <id>293092</id>
    <revision>
      <id>20410204</id>
      <timestamp>2005-08-06T14:33:00Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <username>Ianblair23</username>
        <id>145927</id>
      </contributor>
      <text xml:space="preserve">'''Kaitoke''' is a locality in the southern [[North Island]] of [[New Zealand]]. It is located above the northern end of the [[Hutt Valley]], 40 kilometres northeast of [[Wellington]] and six kilometres from the northern end of [[Upper Hutt]] urban area. It lies close to the southern end of the [[Tararua Ranges]].

The &quot;Puffer&quot; tramping track providing access into the Tararua Forest Park climbs from Kaitoke onto and along the Marchant Ridge. This Ridge is the part of the Tararuas that is prominently visible from Wellington.
The Marchant Track has a side track leading to the Tauherenikau Valley. The end of the Marchant Ridge (after 4 hours of 'up hill, all the way, both ways') leads to Mount Alpha, and onto The Southern Crossing. The entire track to Alpha skirts the Kaitoke Water Catchment at no time entering the catchment. The water supply for Wellington is thus protected from any human contamination.

The first YOUTH HOSTEL of the North Island was established in the old Ministry of Works barracks at Kaitoke, which were situated behind the Post Office and alongside the Kaitoke Hall. The PO, the Hall, and the Youth Hostel were the only buildings of the settlement. 

The Pakuratahi River flows through the eastern Kaitoke Basin from its source in the Rimutaka Ranges. The Railway, which closed with the opening of the Rimutaka Railway Tunnel in 1955, ran from Upper Hutt to Maymorn, to Kaitoke and around Goat Rock up the Pakuratahi to the Summit, and from there, because of excessive steepness, a Fell Mountain Railway system was used to take the trains down to Cross Creek. This railway is now used as a cycling excursion path. Winds can be strong, and more than one train has been blown off the railway lines.

The nearby '''Kaitoke Regional Park''' was the filming location for exterior shots of [[Rivendell]] for the movie ''[[The Lord of the Rings: The Fellowship of the Ring (film)|The Lord of the Rings: The Fellowship of the Ring]]''.

The [[Hutt River, New Zealand|Hutt River]] has its source to the north of Kaitoke, and much of the land in the vicinity is used as a water supply reserve. Filming, rafting, swimming and other human activity all take place downstream of the Wellington City water intake.

[[Category:Wellington Region]]</text>
    </revision>
  </page>
  <page>
    <title>Vladimir Tatlin</title>
    <id>293093</id>
    <revision>
      <id>39163681</id>
      <timestamp>2006-02-11T03:23:12Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <ip>67.174.4.187</ip>
      </contributor>
      <text xml:space="preserve">'''Vladimir Yevgrafovich Tatlin''' (Владимир Евграфович Татлин) ({{OldStyleDate|December 28|1885|December 16}} &amp;ndash; [[May 31]], [[1953]]) worked as a painter and architect.  With [[Kazimir Malevich]] he became one of the two most important figures in the [[Russian avant-garde]] art movement of the 1920s. 

Tatlin was born in [[Kharkiv]], [[Ukraine]], the son of a railway engineer and a poet.  He worked as a merchant sea cadet and spent some time abroad.  He began his art career as an icon painter in [[Moscow]], and attended the [[Moscow School of Painting, Sculpture and Architecture]].

[[Image:TatlinMonument3int.jpg|thumb|none|200px|right|'''Tatlin Tower'''. Model of the ''Monument to the Third International'']] 
Tatlin achieved fame as the architect who designed the huge ''Monument to the Third International'', also known as [[Tatlin's Tower]]. Planned in [[1920]], the monument, was to be a tall tower in iron, glass and steel which would have dwarfed the [[Eiffel Tower]] in [[Paris]] (it was a third taller at 1,300 feet high). Inside the iron-and-steel structure of twin spirals, the design envisaged three building blocks, covered with glass windows, which would rotate at different speeds (the first one, a cube, once a year; the second one, a pyramid, once a month; the third one, a cylinder, once a day). High costs prevented Tatlin from executing the plan.

Tatlin also founded Russian [[Constructivism (art)|Constructivist art]] with his counter-reliefs — structures made of wood and iron for hanging in wall corners. He conceived these [[sculpture]]s in order to question the traditional idea of [[painting]].  

Although close friends at the beginning of their careers, Tatlin and Malevich diverged when Malevich did not agree with the utilitarian program of Constructivism. This led Malevich to develop his [[Suprematism|Suprematist]] program in the city of [[Vitebsk]], where he found a school called [[UNOVIS]] (Champions of the new art). Suprematism came to light in [[1915]] at the 0.10 exhibition, one of the main shows of [[Russian avant-garde]], also called &quot;the last futurist exhibition&quot;.

Tatlin also dedicated himself to the study of clothes, objects and so on. At the end of his life he started to research bird-flight, in order to provide human beings with facilities that would allow them to pursue one of the great dreams of humanity: to fly. 

Tatlin was buried at the [[Novodevichy Cemetery]], [[Russia]].

== See also ==

* [[Tatlin's Tower]]
* [[Konstantin Melnikov]]

[[Category:1885 births|Tatlin, Vladimir]]
[[Category:1953 deaths|Tatlin, Vladimir]]
[[Category:Russian architects|Tatlin, Vladimir]]
[[Category:Russian artists|Tatlin, Vladimir]]
[[Category:Ukrainian painters|Tatlin, Vladimir]]
[[Category:Soviet painters|Tatlin, Vladimir]]
[[Category:Russian avant-garde|Tatlin, Vladimir]]

[[de:Wladimir Jewgrafowitsch Tatlin]]
[[es:Vladimir Tatlin]]
[[fr:Vladimir Tatline]]
[[pl:Władimir Tatlin]]
[[sv:Vladimir Tatlin]]</text>
    </revision>
